# converted from 04-the-cost.md
@title The Cost
@bg kestrel
@mood grief

narrate Kestrel had a fountain too. Corin noticed that, on the way in, and found that he could not remember why it had once made him angry.
narrate They got there on the fourth day. The Fever had been in the city for thirteen.
narrate He did not go to the shrine, or the garrison, or the gate. He went to the middle of the market square, where the fountain was, because the deep place did not care where you stood and Corin had learned, over five years, that it was better to ask for things in places where people could see you do it.
narrate Bryn came with him. The Warden came with him.
narrate He put his marked hand on the rim of the fountain and said, out loud, so that the people in the square could hear him and so that the two men behind him could write it down:
corin neutral I want the Grey Fever out of this city. Every case. Now. All of it.
narrate And the deep place, which does not care what you want and only cares what you ask for, gave him what he asked for.
narrate It arrived the way a debt arrives. Not at a door, with a demand — everywhere at once, as a thing already true.
narrate He felt it in the fingers first, and then the quietness, and then the sense of something being tidied — a room being made neat before guests — and he stood in a market square with his hand on a fountain and felt his own mind being sorted like a drawer.
narrate Four hundred cases. Two thousand people at risk. Nine thousand in the city.
narrate He had asked for all of it and it had been accepted, and the price was being taken, and he stood very still and let it happen, because there was nothing else to do and because a man who flinches at the till has no business in the shop.
narrate It took eleven minutes.
narrate At the end of it, a woman came out of a doorway on the far side of the square carrying a child, and the child was grey, and then the child was not grey, and the woman looked at her own hands and started to scream, and then to laugh, and then the whole square was doing both at once and nobody was looking at Corin at all.
narrate He turned around.
narrate There were two men behind him.
bryn neutral Warden.
narrate One of them was old and grey and wore a coat that had been mended in four places, and Corin knew him — he knew him the way you know a thing you have read about, from the ledger, from the entries he had written himself — and he said
narrate The Warden's face did something complicated.
corin neutral And this is—
narrate Corin looked at the other man. Broad, grey, a coat the colour of stone. Sixty, maybe. A face like weather
narrate He waited.
narrate He waited a long time.
corin neutral I'm sorry,
corin neutral Have we met?
narrate The old man did not move.
narrate He stood in a market square in Kestrel with a fountain running behind him and nine thousand people starting to realise they were going to live, and he looked at Corin with an expression that the Warden — who wrote everything down — would describe, in the ledger that night, as the worst thing I have ever seen on a human face, and I have seen thirty-one villages.
bryn neutral No,
bryn neutral We haven't met.
corin neutral Then why are you here?
bryn neutral I was in the area.
corin neutral ...Right.
corin neutral I'm sorry. I have a condition. I lose things.
narrate Corin looked at him for a moment longer, and there was something — something at the very back, the shape of a thing that had been taken, a smooth quiet space where a whole man used to be
bryn neutral I know,
bryn neutral I've got the book.
narrate They walked out of Kestrel together.
narrate The Warden wrote it all down: the date, the square, the four hundred cases, the eleven minutes, and the exact words Corin had used, and then — because he had promised — the conversation afterwards, verbatim.
narrate Bryn walked on Corin's left, which was where he had walked for five years, and Corin did not notice, and Bryn did not mention it.
narrate They got a mile out of the city before Bryn said anything at all.
corin neutral You asked for the whole city.
bryn neutral Yes.
corin neutral You could have asked for less. A hundred cases. Bought yourself time.
bryn neutral I could have.
corin neutral Why didn't you?
narrate And Corin — who had lost the smell of a cottage, and the woman who gave him bread, and a winter in the Hollow that had never happened, and the name of the only friend he had — thought about it seriously, because it was a serious question, and because the man asking it seemed to deserve a serious answer.
bryn neutral Because I don't know what I'll be next time,
bryn neutral The next time I use it, I might not be able to make the choice. So I made it now, while I still could.
narrate Bryn walked on his left.
bryn neutral That's the arithmetic,
corin neutral It's not the arithmetic. It's the opposite.
bryn neutral I know,
bryn neutral That's what I mean.

